 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering time is dominated by the local rendering phase, which scales with the amount of data per processor. Compositing time is logarithmic with respect to the number of processors ($O(\log P)$) when using tree-based reduction algorithms [S5, unofficial]. Load balancing sorting takes $O(P \log P)$ sequentially or $O(P)$ with parallel merge sort [S6, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Each processor requires memory proportional to the size of its data partition plus the full image buffer ($O(W \times H)$). This can be a bottleneck for very high-resolution displays [S5, S6, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering time is dominated by the local rendering phase, which scales with the amount of data per processor. Compositing time is logarithmic with respect to the number of processors ($O(\log P)$) when using tree-based reduction algorithms [S7]. Load balancing sorting takes $O(P \log P)$ sequentially or $O(P)$ with parallel merge sort [S8, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Each processor requires memory proportional to the size of its data partition plus the full image buffer ($O(W \times H)$). This can be a bottleneck for very high-resolution displays [S7].
 *   **Scalability:** Scalability is heavily bound to network bandwidth. Higher resolutions increase bandwidth requirements, limiting scalability with screen resolution. However, since data can remain stationary, it scales better with increasing volume data size compared to sort-first methods [S3][S5, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Superior load balancing compared to sort-first; reduced communication costs for geometry (only images are transferred, not raw primitives); natural fit for distributed memory systems where data redistribution is expensive [S3][S5, S6, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** High communication overhead during compositing; requires sufficient memory per node for image buffers; transparency handling is complex; performance degrades with unequal primitive sizes in data-parallel regimes if not managed by MIMD [S5, S6, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Large datasets that fit in distributed memory but not single-node memory; scenarios with uneven geometry distribution where load balancing is critical; when high-speed networks are available [S3][S5, S6, unofficial].
